import Lesson from '../../layouts/LessonLayout.astro';

const body = `<h1>2.7b — Character Creator: Display</h1>

<p>
    Your character has stats but no way to show them off. Today you build the sheet —
    including an HP bar, which is a formula wearing a costume.
</p>

<!-- ============================================================ -->
<div class="activity rpg">
    <div class="activity-header">
        <span class="activity-label">RPG Build</span>
        <span class="activity-time">~20 min</span>
    </div>

    <p>
        Keep running <a href="../data/CharacterCheck.java" download>CharacterCheck.java</a>
        as you go — after this method, recompile and watch the <code>FAIL</code> lines shrink.
    </p>

    <h3>Part 4: printCharacterSheet() — Show Your Creation</h3>

    <p><strong>Requirements:</strong></p>
    <ul>
        <li>Method signature: <code>public void printCharacterSheet()</code></li>
        <li>Prints the character's name, an HP bar, and all six ability scores</li>
        <li>
            The HP bar is <strong>exactly 10 characters</strong> wide, sitting between
            <code>[</code> and <code>]</code>: filled blocks first, then empty blocks
        </li>
        <li>An HP line shows current and maximum hit points as <code>hp/maxHp</code></li>
        <li>Use <code>System.out.println()</code> to format each line</li>
        <li>The output must be clean and readable — think about spacing and alignment</li>
    </ul>

    <p><strong>Sample output:</strong></p>
    <pre><code><span class="output">====================
== GROG THE MIGHTY ==
====================
Level 1   HP: [##########] 12/12

STR: 15
DEX: 10
CON: 14
INT:  8
WIS: 11
CHA:  9

AC: 10
Initiative: 0
Passive Perception: 11
Proficiency: +2
====================</span></code></pre>

    <p>
        The exact spacing is up to you — the test checks that the pieces are present and
        that the bar is the right width, not that your sheet is byte-for-byte identical
        to this one.
    </p>

    <div class="socratic">
        <h3>Socratic Checkpoint</h3>
        <ol>
            <li>Where does <code>printCharacterSheet()</code> fit in the three-step
                pattern you have used all unit — build the object, configure it, then
                use it?</li>
            <li>The HP bar shows <code>hp</code> out of <code>maxHp</code>. What would
                happen if those two values got out of sync?</li>
            <li>You are printing six stats, one line each. What is repetitive about it?
                Can you imagine a way to clean this up? (We will explore this with arrays
                in Unit 6.)</li>
        </ol>
    </div>

    <h3>The HP Bar Is a Formula</h3>

    <p>
        A bar that shows what fraction of your hit points remain is a calculation, not a
        decoration. Ten blocks means you are computing <code>hp</code> as a percentage of
        <code>maxHp</code> and scaling it to 10:
    </p>

    <pre><code>int filled = hp * 10 / maxHp;     // correct
int filled = hp / maxHp * 10;     // always 0</code></pre>

    <p>
        That second line is the integer-division trap you met in Unit 1, hiding inside a
        progress bar. At 50 of 100 hit points, <code>50 / 100</code> is <code>0</code>,
        so the second line empties the bar at half health. The first line multiplies
        before dividing and gives the 5 blocks you expect.
    </p>

    <p>Once you have <code>filled</code>, build the bar with <code>substring</code>:</p>

    <pre><code>String full  = "##########";
String empty = "..........";
String bar = full.substring(0, filled) + empty.substring(0, 10 - filled);</code></pre>

    <p>
        <code>substring(0, filled)</code> takes the first <code>filled</code> characters
        of the ten-block string. Two substrings added together always make exactly 10
        characters, however the HP moves.
    </p>

    <div class="tinker">
        <h3>Tinker: The HP Bar</h3>
        <ol>
            <li>
                Work out <code>filled</code> for a character at 50 of 100 hit points. How
                many blocks? Now do it for 12 of 12. Should the bar be completely full at
                full health?
            </li>
            <li>
                Predict first, then test: what does <code>hp / maxHp * 10</code> give at
                50 of 100? Why? Run it and see whether your prediction was right.
            </li>
            <li>
                Your character starts at full health, which makes the bar boring — it is
                always completely full. In <code>CharacterTester.java</code>, write
                <code>c.hp = c.maxHp / 2;</code> and print again. Is the bar about half
                full? If it did not move, your <code>filled</code> is not using
                <code>hp</code>.
            </li>
            <li>
                Your fields are public, so nothing stops you writing
                <code>c.hp = 999;</code> — go on, try it in your tester and see what the
                HP bar does. Now that you have seen the answer: should a class allow
                that? Hold the thought. Unit 5.
            </li>
        </ol>
    </div>

    <div class="callout">
        <strong>Use plain ASCII for the bar.</strong> Blocks (<code>█</code> and
        <code>░</code>) look better, but they are not ASCII, and on some machines
        <code>javac</code> misreads them and turns your 10-character bar into something
        longer — which fails the width check with a confusing message. If you want blocks
        anyway, compile with <code>javac -encoding UTF-8 ...</code>. The test checks the
        bar's <em>width</em>, not which character you chose, so either can pass.
    </div>

    <p><strong>Acceptance Test:</strong></p>
    <pre><code>Character c = new Character();
c.name = "Grog the Mighty";
c.generateStats();
c.printCharacterSheet();</code></pre>
    <p>
        Expected: the name, a completely full bar at full health, and six labeled scores
        between 3 and 18. Then set the HP to half and print again — the bar should be
        about half filled.
    </p>

    <div class="checklist">
        <h3>Checkpoint — before moving on, you must be able to:</h3>
        <ul>
            <li>Build a String of a fixed width from two <code>substring</code> calls</li>
            <li>Scale a value into a range with <code>value * width / maximum</code>, and
                explain why multiplying first matters</li>
            <li>Explain why <code>hp / maxHp * 10</code> always gives 0</li>
            <li>Print a formatted multi-line report from instance fields</li>
        </ul>
    </div>
</div>

<!-- ============================================================ -->
<div class="resources">
    <h3>Reference Docs</h3>
    <ul>
        <li><a href="../docs/unit-02/character-creator.html">Student RPG Guide: Character Creator</a> — full spec</li>
        <li><a href="../docs/unit-02/string.html">String Methods</a> — <code>substring</code> and friends</li>
        <li><a href="../docs/unit-01/casting.html">Casting &amp; Integer Division</a> — the trap in the bar</li>
    </ul>
</div>`;
